1
00:00:01,170 --> 00:00:04,051
So we've covered risks and
how they affect a project,

2
00:00:04,051 --> 00:00:07,847
but we haven't fully discussed
dependencies—arguably one of

3
00:00:07,847 --> 00:00:09,856
the biggest factors in a project.

4
00:00:09,856 --> 00:00:14,340
In this video, we'll discuss
dependencies—both internal and external—how

5
00:00:14,341 --> 00:00:18,007
to identify and track them,
as well as their importance.

6
00:00:18,007 --> 00:00:20,817
There are a few different
types of dependencies, and

7
00:00:20,817 --> 00:00:23,176
we'll discuss a few examples of each one.

8
00:00:23,176 --> 00:00:25,730
So what exactly are dependencies?

9
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:29,561
Dependencies are the links that
connect one project task to another,

10
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:34,380
and as we mentioned, they're often
the greatest source of risk to a project.

11
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:38,954
Two or more project tasks may have
a relationship with one another in which

12
00:00:38,954 --> 00:00:43,528
the completion of one task is reliant
on the initiation of another task, and

13
00:00:43,528 --> 00:00:44,363
vice versa.

14
00:00:44,363 --> 00:00:47,237
Think of these tasks
like a line of dominoes

15
00:00:47,237 --> 00:00:49,970
toppling each other over, one by one.

16
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:54,624
If one domino falls, it'll knock
the next one over and so on and so on.

17
00:00:54,624 --> 00:00:58,970
For instance, a construction company may
have a number of jobs across the city.

18
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:04,091
Each project requires a foreman and
a project manager to be chosen before

19
00:01:04,091 --> 00:01:08,342
the requirements, timeline, and budget
get signed off and the crew is chosen.

20
00:01:08,342 --> 00:01:12,521
You wouldn't choose a crew and tell them
to get to work before the work has been

21
00:01:12,521 --> 00:01:15,113
clearly scoped and
the contracts were signed.

22
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:17,833
That's an example of
an internal dependency,

23
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:22,330
which describes the relationship between
two tasks within the same project.

24
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:28,201
External dependencies, on the other hand,
refer to tasks that are reliant on outside

25
00:01:28,201 --> 00:01:31,303
factors, like regulatory agencies or
other projects.

26
00:01:31,303 --> 00:01:35,477
For instance, if a construction company
is scheduled to demolish a building site,

27
00:01:35,477 --> 00:01:38,789
they'll have to wait until their
project is approved by the city.

28
00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:42,811
External dependencies aren't always
in the project manager's control, but

29
00:01:42,811 --> 00:01:46,590
it's important to be aware of them so
that the project stays on track.

30
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:50,762
Mandatory dependencies are tasks that
are legally or contractually required.

31
00:01:50,762 --> 00:01:54,872
For instance, when that construction
company finishes the demolition and

32
00:01:54,872 --> 00:01:58,917
starts the rebuild, they'll first have
to pour a concrete foundation and

33
00:01:58,917 --> 00:02:01,676
then have it inspected by
the city to ensure it meets

34
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:05,682
their standards before the construction
company can continue to build.

35
00:02:05,682 --> 00:02:10,011
Lastly, discretionary dependencies
are defined by the project team.

36
00:02:10,011 --> 00:02:13,208
These are dependencies that
could occur on their own, but

37
00:02:13,208 --> 00:02:17,071
the team saw a need to make those
dependencies reliant on one another.

38
00:02:17,071 --> 00:02:21,836
For instance, the construction company may
be using concrete from a new supplier and

39
00:02:21,836 --> 00:02:22,908
want to run a test,

40
00:02:22,908 --> 00:02:26,942
pouring a portion of the foundation to
get a better estimate of the total amount

41
00:02:26,942 --> 00:02:31,158
of product they'll need to complete the
foundation, rather than buying too much or

42
00:02:31,158 --> 00:02:32,705
too little product up front.

43
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:35,903
The task of pouring a portion
of the foundation comes first,

44
00:02:35,903 --> 00:02:39,552
because the team needed more
information before making a decision.

45
00:02:39,552 --> 00:02:44,460
A project manager has to work diligently
to incorporate dependency management.

46
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:49,227
Dependency management is the process of
managing all of these interrelated tasks

47
00:02:49,227 --> 00:02:53,511
and resources within the project
to ensure that your overall project is

48
00:02:53,511 --> 00:02:56,293
completed successfully on time and
in budget.

49
00:02:56,293 --> 00:02:58,413
To pursue effective dependency management,

50
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:01,690
there are four important steps
that a project manager can take:

51
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:06,063
proper identification, recording
dependencies, continuous monitoring and

52
00:03:06,063 --> 00:03:08,255
control, and efficient communication.

53
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:11,622
The first step is proper identification.

54
00:03:11,622 --> 00:03:15,466
A project manager will have to
brainstorm all possible project

55
00:03:15,466 --> 00:03:19,463
dependencies with their team and
categorize them accordingly.

56
00:03:19,463 --> 00:03:22,243
Next step is recording dependencies.

57
00:03:22,243 --> 00:03:27,010
After all dependencies are identified,
a risk register should be created.

58
00:03:27,010 --> 00:03:31,304
A risk register is a table or
chart that contains your list of risks and

59
00:03:31,304 --> 00:03:32,351
dependencies.

60
00:03:32,351 --> 00:03:36,132
The risk register should include
a description of the dependency,

61
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:41,350
the date, and all activities or tasks
that may be impacted by the dependency.

62
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:45,809
Then, a project manager will want to
maintain continuous monitoring and

63
00:03:45,809 --> 00:03:46,485
control.

64
00:03:46,485 --> 00:03:50,197
This means you will want to schedule
regular meetings to check in on

65
00:03:50,197 --> 00:03:51,666
the interrelated tasks,

66
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:55,581
staying up-to-date on any progress
being made and double checking for

67
00:03:55,581 --> 00:03:57,854
changes that will impact other tasks.

68
00:03:57,854 --> 00:04:00,462
Last step is efficient communication.

69
00:04:00,462 --> 00:04:05,078
Keeping the project team and stakeholders
updated can help resolve dependencies and

70
00:04:05,078 --> 00:04:07,450
keep the project going strong.

71
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:12,643
To recap, we just discussed how to define
dependencies, both internal and external,

72
00:04:12,643 --> 00:04:16,658
and we learned the importance of
managing and tracking dependencies.

73
00:04:16,658 --> 00:04:20,875
We also discussed the importance of
clearly defining dependencies at

74
00:04:20,875 --> 00:04:24,162
the project outset,
like in our foundation example,

75
00:04:24,162 --> 00:04:26,811
and learned about dependency management.

76
00:04:26,811 --> 00:04:27,819
In the next video,

77
00:04:27,819 --> 00:04:31,730
we'll give you concrete techniques
to manage the risks in your project.

78
00:04:31,730 --> 00:04:32,876
I'll meet you there.
